A simple way to get a valid peak is to find the largest value in the whole matrix. The global maximum cannot have a larger neighbor, so it is greater than every valid neighbor around it.

For example, the largest value in `[[1, 4], [3, 2]]` is `4` at `[0, 1]`, which is a peak. This is not the fastest method for a large grid, but it is very easy to check. The input matrix is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findPeakGrid(vector<vector<int>>& mat) {
        int bestRow = 0;
        int bestCol = 0;

        for (int row = 0; row < static_cast<int>(mat.size()); row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < static_cast<int>(mat[0].size()); col++) {
                if (mat[row][col] > mat[bestRow][bestCol]) {
                    bestRow = row;
                    bestCol = col;
                }
            }
        }

        return {bestRow, bestCol};
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The code below checks cells directly and returns the first one that is larger than its four neighbors. Missing neighbors are treated as `-1`, matching the grid boundary rule.

The corner and one-row cases are handled by the same neighbor checks. This version does not modify `mat`, and it is useful when keeping the logic more direct matters more than reducing the scan time.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] findPeakGrid(int[][] mat) {
        int rows = mat.length;
        int cols = mat[0].length;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                int current = mat[row][col];
                int up = row > 0 ? mat[row - 1][col] : -1;
                int down = row + 1 < rows ? mat[row + 1][col] : -1;
                int left = col > 0 ? mat[row][col - 1] : -1;
                int right = col + 1 < cols ? mat[row][col + 1] : -1;

                if (current > up && current > down &&
                    current > left && current > right) {
                    return new int[]{row, col};
                }
            }
        }

        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)` because each cell may be checked.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version searches over rows instead of columns. In the selected row, it finds the largest element. Since that element is the row maximum, its left and right neighbors cannot be larger. Only the top and bottom neighbors decide which half of the rows remains.

The returned position is valid when both vertical neighbors are smaller. Boundary rows use `-1` for the missing neighbor, so a single-row matrix is covered too. The matrix stays unchanged.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] findPeakGrid(int[][] mat) {
        int rows = mat.length;
        int cols = mat[0].length;
        int low = 0;
        int high = rows - 1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int middle = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int maxCol = 0;

            for (int col = 1; col < cols; col++) {
                if (mat[middle][col] > mat[middle][maxCol]) {
                    maxCol = col;
                }
            }

            int current = mat[middle][maxCol];
            int top = middle > 0 ? mat[middle - 1][maxCol] : -1;
            int bottom = middle + 1 < rows ? mat[middle + 1][maxCol] : -1;

            if (current > top && current > bottom) {
                return new int[]{middle, maxCol};
            }

            if (top > current) {
                high = middle - 1;
            } else {
                low = middle + 1;
            }
        }

        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(columns * log(rows))`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Starting at the top-left cell, this version always moves to a larger adjacent value. Values strictly increase after every move, so the search cannot loop. When no neighbor is larger, the current cell is a peak.

A quick dry run on `[[1, 2], [3, 4]]` moves from `1` to `3`, then to `4`, and stops at `[1, 1]`. The method handles corners by using `-1` outside the matrix. It does not change the input, although its worst case can visit many cells, unlike the binary search versions.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findPeakGrid(vector<vector<int>>& mat) {
        int rows = mat.size();
        int cols = mat[0].size();
        int row = 0;
        int col = 0;

        while (true) {
            int bestRow = row;
            int bestCol = col;
            int current = mat[row][col];

            if (row > 0 && mat[row - 1][col] > mat[bestRow][bestCol]) {
                bestRow = row - 1;
                bestCol = col;
            }
            if (row + 1 < rows && mat[row + 1][col] > mat[bestRow][bestCol]) {
                bestRow = row + 1;
                bestCol = col;
            }
            if (col > 0 && mat[row][col - 1] > mat[bestRow][bestCol]) {
                bestRow = row;
                bestCol = col - 1;
            }
            if (col + 1 < cols && mat[row][col + 1] > mat[bestRow][bestCol]) {
                bestRow = row;
                bestCol = col + 1;
            }

            if (bestRow == row && bestCol == col) {
                return {row, col};
            }

            row = bestRow;
            col = bestCol;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)` in the worst case, since the path can be long.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

