## Intuition
The Intuition
Instead of scanning every element (O(n.m)), we treat the columns as our search space.

Peak in a Row: By always picking the maximum element in a chosen column, we guarantee that this element is already greater than its top and bottom neighbors (since we picked the largest). This allows us to focus solely on the left and right neighbors, effectively reducing the 2D problem to a 1D binary search scenario.
Elimination: If the current maximum in a column is smaller than its left neighbor, we know a peak must exist in the left half. If it's smaller than its right neighbor, a peak exists in the right half 
## Approach
Perform binary search on the columns, with low = 0 and high = m - 1 
Calculate mid column. Find the row index of the maximum element in that mid column .
Compare this element with its immediate left and right neighbors 
If it is greater than both, it is a peak; return its coordinates 
If the left element is greater, discard the right half of the search space (high = mid - 1); otherwise, discard the left half (low = mid + 1).
Edge Cases
Boundaries: The problem treats elements outside the matrix boundaries as -1 
Single Row/Column: The algorithm handles these naturally because the logic checking neighbors against the boundaries (-1) remains consistent 
Multiple Peaks: Since the problem accepts any valid peak, the binary search will converge to one of the multiple existing peaks based on the path taken through the matrix 

